import java.util.*;

public class MapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();

        // Adding elements to HashMap
        hashMap.put("apple", 42);
        hashMap.put("banana", 30);

        // Adding elements to TreeMap
        treeMap.put("apple", 42);
        treeMap.put("banana", 30);

        System.out.println("HashMap: " + hashMap);
        System.out.println("TreeMap: " + treeMap);
    }
}
```

When deciding which to use, consider the trade-offs between speed and order. If you need fast access and do not require the keys to be sorted, `HashMap` is a good choice. If you need the keys to be sorted and can tolerate slightly slower access times, `TreeMap` might be more suitable.

